# SECURITY HEADER OWNERSHIP (Sprint B 2026-05-13)
# ------------------------------------------------
# _headers owns:        X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options,
#                       Referrer-Policy, all caching rules.
# Edge function owns:   Content-Security-Policy, Permissions-Policy,
#                       Strict-Transport-Security.
#                       (see netlify/edge-functions/security-headers.js)
#
# Why split: the edge function runs on EVERY route (including dynamic
# Netlify Function routes where _headers doesn't apply). Caching + the
# non-CSP security headers stay here because they're more efficient
# at the static-file layer. Previously CSP + Permissions-Policy were
# duplicated across both files with subtle differences (jsdelivr only
# in _headers but unused anywhere; *.stripe.com wildcard in _headers
# vs explicit subdomains in edge); Netlify's pipeline applies _headers
# first then the edge function can override, so the edge always won
# for CSP. Now de-duplicated and documented.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/styles/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/js/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

# Extension-based cache rules (images / HTML / fonts) live in
# netlify.toml [[headers]] (for = "*.jpg" etc.). Netlify's _headers
# parser does NOT accept bare-extension globs — they were silently
# rejected here as the "2 invalid header rules" advisory, and the
# leading-slash form (/*.jpg) fails the deploy outright. netlify.toml's
# `for` matcher is the supported home for extension-based rules.

# Root path and extensionless pretty URLs. Netlify serves these by
# rewriting to the corresponding .html file internally, so the HTML
# cache rule (in netlify.toml) does NOT apply to them. Set explicit
# no-cache here so edge caches always revalidate these routes.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/index
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Deploy marker: always serve fresh. This is the single source of
# truth for "which commit is currently live" (see build.js deploy-id
# block). If this file is cached, the verifier can't tell whether
# the latest deploy landed.
/deploy-id.txt
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/feed.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
